{
  "values": {
    "gallery.html": {
      "The gallery gathers images used in Saint Charbel devotion: a crowd scene, a sepia portrait, and an image shared with a testimony account. Click any image to open it full-screen and move through the collection with the arrow keys.": 1,
      "About This Archive": 1,
      "This gallery brings together three images used on the site: a black-and-white scene of a crowd gathered around a monk, the familiar sepia devotional portrait, and an image shared with an apparition testimony. The links below each image show the credited source. The testimony image is presented as part of that account, not independent proof of an apparition.": 1,
      "Three photographs, each with its source linked in the Image Sources section above.": 1,
      "Devotional and testimony images, with source links.": 1,
      "Full-screen viewing with keyboard navigation: click an image, then use the arrow keys.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "What is in the gallery?": 1,
      "Three images used in Saint Charbel devotion: a crowd scene, a sepia devotional portrait, and an image shared with an apparition testimony. Each image has a credited source link on the page.": 1,
      "Where do the images come from?": 1,
      "Every image has its source listed in the Image Sources section on this page, with a link, so you can see where each photograph was found.": 1,
      "How do I view an image full-screen?": 1,
      "Click any image to open the full view, then move through the collection with the Previous and Next buttons or the arrow keys on your keyboard.": 1,
      "Can I reuse these photographs?": 1,
      "A source credit is not permission to reuse an image. Check the rights with the source before copying or sharing it.": 1
    },
    "jpii-story.html": {
      "Saint Storybooks": 1,
      "Story Summary": 1,
      "This read-aloud storybook tells children ages 5 to 12 the true story of John Paul II: the boy Karol Wojtyla, called Lolek, from Wadowice in Poland, who lost his family one person at a time, kept his faith alive through war, studied for the priesthood in secret, and became the first non-Italian pope in 455 years. It ends with the three words he gave the world - do not be afraid - and with the forgiveness he showed the man who shot him.": 1,
      "For ages 5 to 12, with read-aloud narration on every page.": 1,
      "An 11-page storybook told in simple words.": 1,
      "Grounded in the documented record; the fuller adult account is on the": 1,
      "St John Paul II": 1,
      "page.": 1,
      "Part of the": 1,
      "library.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Who is this storybook for?": 1,
      "Children ages 5 to 12, reading on their own or listening with family. Every page has a Read Aloud button with gentle narration, so the book can be heard as well as read.": 1,
      "Who was John Paul II?": 1,
      "Karol Wojtyla (1920-2005), a Polish cardinal elected pope in 1978 - the first non-Italian pope in 455 years. He carried the Gospel to 129 countries and was canonized on April 27, 2014.": 1,
      "Is the story true?": 1,
      "Yes. It retells documented events: his boyhood in Wadowice, the secret seminary during the war, his election as pope in 1978, and his forgiveness of the man who shot him.": 1,
      "Why did he say \"do not be afraid\"?": 1,
      "He spoke those words at the start of his papacy in 1978, to a world that had lived through wars and dictatorships. He meant that trust in Christ is stronger than fear.": 1
    },
    "mother-teresa-story.html": {
      "Story Summary": 1,
      "This read-aloud storybook tells children ages 5 to 12 the true story of Mother Teresa: the girl Gonxha from Skopje who became a teacher in India, heard what she called a call within a call on a train to Darjeeling in 1946, and went into the slums of Calcutta to care for the poorest of the poor. It tells of the sisters who joined her, the work that grew around the world, and the long hidden darkness she carried in prayer.": 1,
      "What This Story Covers": 1,
      "Gonxha in Skopje": 1,
      "A Teacher in India": 1,
      "A Call on the Train": 1,
      "Learning to Care": 1,
      "Others Join Her": 1,
      "Many Hands at Work": 1,
      "When Prayer Felt Dark": 1,
      "A Saint Remembered": 1,
      "Who Is Before You?": 1,
      "Points of reflection": 1,
      "The grown-up account, with sources and her own words, is on the": 1,
      "St Teresa of Calcutta": 2,
      "page.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Who was Mother Teresa?": 1,
      "Born Agnes Gonxhe Bojaxhiu in Skopje in 1910, she founded the Missionaries of Charity in Calcutta, received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979, and was canonized on September 4, 2016.": 1,
      "Who is this storybook for?": 1,
      "Children ages 5 to 12, reading on their own or listening with family. Every page has a Read Aloud button with gentle narration, so the book can be heard as well as read.": 1,
      "Is the story true?": 1,
      "Yes. It retells documented events. The book's own notes name the reported interior experiences, like the call on the train, as reported experiences, and explain that some ordinary scenes are composites.": 1,
      "Where can I read more about Mother Teresa?": 1,
      "The": 1,
      "page on this site tells her full story for adults, with sources and her own words.": 1
    },
    "mystery-meditation.html": {
      "This page is a guided meditation on the Rosary mysteries for today. It shows each mystery with its fruit and Scripture reference, reads the prayers aloud with a hands-free timer, and lists the full prayer texts so you can read and pray along.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Which mysteries are prayed today?": 1,
      "By the weekday tradition: the Joyful mysteries on Monday and Saturday, the Sorrowful on Tuesday and Friday, the Luminous on Thursday, and the Glorious on Wednesday and Sunday.": 1,
      "What does this page include?": 1,
      "The day's mystery set with each mystery's fruit and Scripture reference, voice narration with a hands-free timer, and the full texts of the main Rosary prayers so you can read and pray along.": 1,
      "Can I pray a different mystery?": 1,
      "Yes. Use the Previous and Next buttons to move through all twenty mysteries in order, or open a specific mystery from the Prayer Library.": 1,
      "What is the fruit of a mystery?": 1,
      "Each mystery is paired with a virtue to ask for while praying it - for example, humility and surrender to God for the first Joyful mystery, the Annunciation.": 1
    },
    "pio-story.html": {
      "Saint Storybooks": 1,
      "Story Summary": 1,
      "This read-aloud storybook tells children ages 5 to 12 the true story of Padre Pio: the boy Francesco Forgione from Pietrelcina who learned to pray, became a Capuchin friar and priest, and spent more than fifty years at San Giovanni Rotondo listening to people's sorrows. It tells, gently and honestly, of the wounds he bore, the hospital he built for the sick, and the counsel he left the world: pray, hope, and don't worry.": 1,
      "For ages 5 to 12, with read-aloud narration on every page.": 1,
      "A 16-page storybook told in simple words.": 1,
      "Grounded in the documented record; the fuller adult account is on the": 1,
      "St Padre Pio": 1,
      "page.": 1,
      "Part of the": 1,
      "library.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Who is this storybook for?": 1,
      "Children ages 5 to 12, reading on their own or listening with family. Every page has a Read Aloud button with gentle narration, so the book can be heard as well as read.": 1,
      "Who was Padre Pio?": 1,
      "A Capuchin friar from southern Italy (1887-1968) who heard confessions for up to sixteen hours a day, bore wounds like those of Christ for fifty years, and founded a hospital at San Giovanni Rotondo. The Church canonized him on June 16, 2002.": 1,
      "Is the story true?": 1,
      "Yes. It retells documented events of his life. The harder chapters, like the wounds he bore, are told honestly and gently, and the adult page separates what is documented from what is reported.": 1,
      "What does \"pray, hope, and don't worry\" mean?": 1,
      "It was Padre Pio's short counsel for anxious people: bring everything to God in prayer, trust Him with the outcome, and refuse to carry tomorrow's fear today.": 1
    },
    "rafqa-story.html": {
      "Story Summary": 1,
      "This read-aloud storybook tells children ages 5 to 12 the true story of Saint Rafqa: the girl Boutroussieh from the village of Himlaya who chose a life of prayer, taught children, once hid a child under her robe to keep him safe, and bore years of blindness and illness with patience. It closes with the sisters who cared for her at Jrabta and with the Church remembering her as Lebanon's first canonized woman.": 1,
      "What This Story Covers": 1,
      "A Child in Himlaya": 1,
      "Her Own Choice": 1,
      "Work and Learning": 1,
      "Courage in Deir el-Kamar": 1,
      "A New Name": 1,
      "Care in Illness": 1,
      "The Sisters at Jrabta": 1,
      "A Saint Remembered": 1,
      "One Kind Step": 1,
      "Points of reflection": 1,
      "The grown-up account, with sources, her prayers, and her shrine, is on the": 1,
      "St Rafqa": 2,
      "page.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Who was Saint Rafqa?": 1,
      "A Lebanese nun (1832-1914) of the same Maronite order that formed Saint Charbel, and the first woman of Lebanon to be canonized. Blind and largely paralyzed in her last years, she stayed patient and thankful. Her shrine at Jrabta still draws pilgrims.": 1,
      "Who is this storybook for?": 1,
      "Children ages 5 to 12, reading on their own or listening with family. Every page has a Read Aloud button with gentle narration, so the book can be heard as well as read.": 1,
      "Is the story true?": 1,
      "Yes. It retells documented events. Her reported inner experiences, like what she felt before the icon and her dream, are named as reported experiences in the book's own notes.": 1,
      "Where can I read more about Saint Rafqa?": 1,
      "The": 1,
      "page on this site tells her full story for adults, with sources, her prayers, and her shrine at Jrabta.": 1
    },
    "rosary-intro.html": {
      "The Rosary opens with the Apostles' Creed, one Our Father, three Hail Marys for faith, hope, and charity, and the Glory Be. This page starts you with those opening prayers, then walks you through all five mysteries of the set you choose - or today's set, picked for you by the weekday tradition - with the prayers read along automatically.": 1,
      "The Opening Prayers, Step by Step": 1,
      "Every Rosary begins the same way. You mark yourself with the Sign of the Cross, then say the Apostles' Creed - the short statement of what Christians believe. One Our Father follows, then three Hail Marys offered for faith, hope, and charity, and the Glory Be. Only then does the first decade begin.": 1,
      "Each decade keeps the same shape: the mystery is announced, one Our Father, ten Hail Marys prayed slowly with the mystery in mind, and the Glory Be. The Fatima prayer closes each decade. When the fifth decade ends, the Rosary closes with the Hail Holy Queen and the concluding Rosary prayer.": 1,
      "Opening: Sign of the Cross, Apostles' Creed, Our Father, three Hail Marys, Glory Be.": 1,
      "Each decade: the mystery, Our Father, ten Hail Marys, Glory Be, Fatima prayer.": 1,
      "Close: Hail Holy Queen and the concluding Rosary prayer.": 1,
      "The recommended mode picks today's mystery set by the weekday tradition.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "What prayers open the Rosary?": 1,
      "The Sign of the Cross and the Apostles' Creed, then one Our Father, three Hail Marys offered for faith, hope, and charity, and the Glory Be. After these opening prayers the first decade begins.": 1,
      "Why three Hail Marys at the start?": 1,
      "They are offered for the virtues of faith, hope, and charity - a way of asking Mary to help you pray the mysteries well before the first decade begins.": 1,
      "What is the Fatima prayer?": 1,
      "A short prayer said after each decade: \"O my Jesus, forgive us our sins, save us from the fires of hell; lead all souls to Heaven, especially those in most need of Thy mercy.\"": 1,
      "How does this page choose today's mysteries?": 1,
      "By the weekday tradition: the Joyful mysteries on Monday and Saturday, the Sorrowful on Tuesday and Friday, the Luminous on Thursday, and the Glorious on Wednesday and Sunday.": 1
    },
    "rosary-minibook.html": {
      "The Rosary Minibook gathers the full text companion for every mystery in one place: an explanation of each mystery, a Scripture reading summary, and a reflection, organized for direct prayer use. Tap a mystery to open its guided page with synced voice and written text.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "What is the Rosary Minibook?": 1,
      "A text companion for praying the Rosary: every mystery with an explanation, a Scripture reading summary, and a reflection, organized for direct prayer use, plus the Fifteen Promises of the Rosary in one place.": 1,
      "What are the Fifteen Promises of the Rosary?": 1,
      "Promises passed down through the Dominican tradition, attributed to the Virgin Mary in private revelations to Saint Dominic and Blessed Alan de Rupe. They are devotional tradition, not a formal decree of the Church, and not a mechanical guarantee - an encouragement to perseverance, never a bargain.": 1,
      "How do I use the minibook while praying?": 1,
      "Tap a mystery link in the Mystery Text Library to open its guided page, where the written text and the voice stay in sync, so you can read and listen together.": 1,
      "Can I read one specific mystery?": 1,
      "Yes. The Mystery Text Library lists every one of the twenty mysteries - four sets of five - and each link opens that mystery's own guided page.": 1
    },
    "rosary-prayer-coach.html": {
      "The Rosary Prayer Coach builds a calm guided Rosary session around the time you have and the intention you bring. Choose five, ten, or twenty minutes, pick a mystery set, and it leads you through each prayer slowly, with short lines, gentle pauses, and no pressure.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "What does the Rosary Prayer Coach do?": 1,
      "It builds a guided Rosary session around the time you have and the intention you bring, then leads you through each prayer step by step, at a slow pace, with short lines and gentle pauses.": 1,
      "How long can a session be?": 1,
      "Five, ten, or twenty minutes - you choose at the start, and the coach shapes the session to fit.": 1,
      "What is guided mode like?": 1,
      "Slow and unhurried: short lines, gentle pauses, and prompts like \"When you're ready\" and \"Take a slow breath.\" Scripture lines are paraphrased briefly for meditation.": 1,
      "What is the shape of the prayers in a session?": 1,
      "The Sign of the Cross, Apostles' Creed, Our Father, three Hail Marys, and Glory Be to open; then for each decade the mystery is announced, one Our Father and ten Hail Marys with meditation, the Glory Be, and an optional Fatima prayer; a closing prayer and the Sign of the Cross end the session.": 1
    },
    "stories.html": {
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "What are the Saint Storybooks?": 1,
      "Read-aloud storybooks for children ages 6 to 12, covering saints including Saint Charbel, Padre Pio, John Paul II, Saint Rafqa, and Mother Teresa. Each book opens at the first page and reads to the end, page by page.": 1,
      "What ages are the storybooks for?": 1,
      "Ages 6 to 12 - young enough to listen with family, old enough to read alone. The words are simple, and every page can be listened to as well as read.": 1,
      "Do the storybooks read aloud?": 1,
      "Yes. Every page has gentle narration: press Read Aloud, or start at the first page and let the book read itself to the end.": 1,
      "Are the stories true?": 1,
      "Each book retells the documented events of its saint's life. Where a story includes a reported inner experience or a traditional account, the book's own notes say so plainly.": 1
    },
    "story.html": {
      "Saint Storybooks": 1,
      "Story Summary": 1,
      "This read-aloud storybook tells the true story of Saint Charbel Makhlouf for children ages 5 to 12. It follows the boy from Bekaa Kafra who lost his father young, chose a life of silence and prayer, became a monk in 1851 and a priest in 1859, and lived his last twenty-three years as the hermit of Annaya. It closes with his death in 1898 and his canonization in 1977, and it shows, in simple words, why people around the world still ask for his prayers.": 1,
      "For ages 5 to 12, with read-aloud narration on every page.": 1,
      "A 27-page storybook told in simple words.": 1,
      "Retells documented events; where the telling carries a traditional account, such as the water lamp, the book says so.": 1,
      "Part of the": 1,
      "library.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "Who is this storybook for?": 1,
      "Children ages 5 to 12, reading on their own or listening with family. Every page has a Read Aloud button with gentle narration, so the book can be heard as well as read.": 1,
      "Is the story of Saint Charbel true?": 1,
      "Yes. The storybook retells documented events of his life - his birth in Bekaa Kafra in 1828, his years as a monk and hermit, and his death in 1898. Where the book shares a traditional account, such as the water lamp, it names it as a traditional account.": 1,
      "What is Saint Charbel known for?": 1,
      "He was a Lebanese monk and the hermit of Annaya, remembered for a hidden life of prayer and for the healings linked to his intercession after his death. The Church canonized him on October 9, 1977.": 1,
      "How long is the story?": 1,
      "The storybook has 27 pages. You can move through them with the Previous and Next buttons.": 1
    },
    "videos.html": {
      ".": 1,
      "Every video here is hosted on YouTube by the channel that made it - we did not produce them and cannot vouch for every word in every report. Where a video tells a miracle story, our own pages keep the careful distinctions: what the Church has formally recognized, what is recorded at the monastery, and what remains a personal testimony. Embeds use youtube-nocookie.com, using YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed mode.": 1,
      "Frequently Asked Questions": 1,
      "What videos are on this page?": 1,
      "Documentaries on Saint Charbel's life, from a short ten-minute film to a full eighty-minute documentary, the complete feature film with English subtitles, the older six-part film, miracle reports, and footage from Annaya and the relic tours.": 1,
      "Are the videos hosted on this site?": 1,
      "No. They are embedded from YouTube through the youtube-nocookie player, using YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed mode.": 1,
      "Can I watch the full Saint Charbel movie here?": 1,
      "Yes. The full feature film about Saint Charbel's life, with English subtitles, is embedded on this page - about one hour and forty-four minutes.": 1,
      "Where can I read the story behind a video?": 1,
      "The site also has written context on the": 1,
      "Miracles pages": 1,
      ", the": 1,
      "pilgrimage guide": 1,
      ", and the": 1,
      "relics page": 1
    }
